\ dip splicing under the lock: the literal quotation compiles inline against
\ the caller's typed stack with the retained entry set aside, so the `+` runs
\ on unboxed operands and no standalone quotation body is reified. The two
\ `.` calls splice as compound bodies and their `inspect` monomorphizes on the
\ proven fixnums. Before the splicer this program could not build locked.

